// @no-instantiate
// The redundant-annotation HINT is the defect's only signpost in the tree, so it is declared
// here rather than suppressed — a reader who follows it writes the spelling that WORKS.
// @hint redundant type annotation: `f` is inferred as `(i32) => "a" | "b"` — remove it to lean on inference
// (No message pattern on the directive, deliberately: the two engines that run this file
// word the same rejection differently — wasmtime says `type mismatch: expected i32, found
// (ref $type)` and V8 says `call[0] expected type i32, found call_indirect of type (ref 2)`
// — so a pattern either pins one engine or degenerates to a substring both happen to share.)
// MISCOMPILE, pinned: CALLING a function VALUE whose annotated type declares an INLINE
// string literal-union RESULT. `vl check` exits 0 (with a HINT that the annotation is
// redundant, which is the only thing in the tree that points at it) and is the ONLY command
// that misses it — `vl build` writes the `.wasm` and then exits 1, as does
// `vl check --codegen`. The engine refuses the module at load —
//
//   type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)
//
// THE MECHANISM IS `ctxKeepsLitUnion`'S OWN DOCUMENTED ASYMMETRY, reached. That predicate
// PRESERVES a literal union's members at `RC_FN_RES` and SOFTENS them at `RC_ROOT`, so the
// two halves of this program disagree about one type:
//
//   function toAB(x: i32): "a" | "b"          // a DECLARATION — `RC_ROOT`, result is a
//                                             // string ref, and the emitted functype says so
//   const f: (i32) => ("a" | "b") = toAB      // a function TYPE — `RC_FN_RES`, result is the
//                                             // interned i32 ATOM, and the `$fnsig` says so
//
// The store is fine (see the control grid below — an annotated-but-never-called binding
// runs); it is the `call_ref` RESULT that meets the disagreement, pushing a `(ref $string)`
// where the interned signature promised an i32.
//
// `compiler/typecheck.vl` STATES THIS ASYMMETRY AND SAYS IT IS UNREACHABLE — "that asymmetry
// is untouched here and is not reachable as a miscompile — the soundness floor refuses the
// shape ('function-value call arity has no interned signature') identically before and
// after, measured both ways". That claim is what this file retracts. It holds for no arity
// measured here: the ZERO-argument spelling the comment quotes is invalid wasm too, not a
// refusal. The comment is corrected in place; this file is what stops it drifting back.
//
// THE GRID. Every cell below was RUN, none inferred. The five RUNNING ones are pinned
// executably in `tests/cases/literal-unions/fntype-litunion-result-controls.vl`; of the three
// INVALID ones only the first is pinned — the other two are the same defect through a
// different spelling and would go green with it, so they are recorded rather than duplicated:
//
//   const f: (i32) => ("a" | "b") = toAB   then `f(1)`  → invalid module   ← this file
//   const f: (i32) => "a" | "b"   = toAB   then `f(1)`  → invalid module (unparenthesized)
//   const f: () => ("a" | "b")    = mk     then `f()`   → invalid module (ZERO-arg)
//   const f                       = toAB   then `f(1)`  → runs, `b`   (no annotation)
//   const f: (i32) => K           = toK    then `f(1)`  → runs        (a declared ALIAS:
//                                                        `K` reps as the atom at BOTH
//                                                        positions, so nothing disagrees)
//   function apply(g: (i32) => ("a" | "b"), …)          → runs, `b`   (a PARAMETER, not a
//                                                        binding)
//   const f: (i32) => (1 | 2)     = to12   then `f(1)`  → runs, `2`   (NUMERIC — not an
//                                                        interned atom in either position)
//   const f: (i32) => ("a" | "b") = toAB   never called → runs        (the STORE is fine)
//
// AND ONE GENERIC NEIGHBOUR, found by the std review of the branch that filed this row and
// recorded here rather than pinned separately, because it is the same disagreement reached
// through a type PARAMETER instead of an annotation:
//
//   function once<A>(f: (A) => A, init: A): A { return f(init) }
//   over `type K = "a" | "b"`                            → invalid module
//   the same with `init: A` declared FIRST               → runs, `b`
//
// It matters to `std/array.vl`, whose surviving carve-out is the sibling shape (`reduce`'s
// accumulator) and which describes that one as an HONEST emit refusal. It is honest for
// `reduce`; this cell shows the defect underneath is not honest at every arity, which is why
// that header now says so explicitly.
//
// ONE CAUTION ON READING THAT GRID, learned by writing the controls file: the alias cell has
// to use a DISJOINT member set. A same-layout alias declared anywhere in the module graph
// changes how the INLINE spelling reps — `std/array.vl`'s "the hazard scope is the compiled
// program, not the module" — so a controls file whose alias was also `"a" | "b"` turned
// three of its own running cells into `emitProgram: literal-union string widening with no
// members`. That interaction is a SEPARATE row with no witness yet, and it is not this one.
//
// So the predicate is: an INLINE STRING literal union as the RESULT of an annotated function
// TYPE that is CALLED through a binding. The alias spelling is the workaround, and it is the
// spelling the checker's redundant-annotation hint effectively steers a reader toward
// anyway.
//
// WHEN IT IS FIXED this file starts failing (the module instantiates), which is the point:
// delete it, fold its row into the controls file beside it, and re-check
// `ctxKeepsLitUnion`'s header — it is the home of the rule this row is about.
function toAB(x: i32): "a" | "b" {
  if x > 0 { return "b" }
  "a"
}

const f: (i32) => ("a" | "b") = toAB
print(f(1))
